{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787061316,"nonce":"jDC4SvFIR+4/TXo7IHhmGUJ7Z84=","title":"`alert_groups` is named for what it contains rather than what it is for, and it reads as a peer of `alert_cases` when it is a different kind of thing","message":"**What is wrong.** `alert_groups` says \"a group of alerts\", which is true and uninformative. Sitting beside\n`alerts`, `alert_cases`, `alert_labels`, `alert_events` and `alert_snoozes` — all of which are facts ABOUT\none alert — it reads as another such fact, when it is a grouping of many alerts existing for a different\npurpose entirely. The owner has said plainly the name confuses him, and he is the second reader to be\nconfused by it today.\n\n**The demonstration.** In this same session a nav item labelled \"Cases\" was wired to `listAlertGroups`, and\nneither the author nor the reviewer caught it from the name alone — the table sounds enough like a sibling of\n`alert_cases` that showing one under the other's label looked plausible. Separately, the repo's naming\nconvention is entity-family, not module (`alert_*`, `notification_*`, `channel_*`), and this table is the one\nmember of the `alert_*` family that is not a fact about an alert.\n\n**Why it matters.** This is the object that owns a Slack thread and decides what an operator is told about\ntogether. A name that does not say so means every reader has to open `00008_grouping.sql` to learn what it\nis for, and the two objects most easily confused in the product — the thing you acknowledge and the thing\nthat owns the conversation — are named as if they were the same kind of thing.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/00008_grouping.sql:15` · `internal/grouping/**` (sole writer:\n`repository/group.go`) · six persisted event types `group.opened|closed|member_joined|member_left|storm_started|storm_ended`\n(`internal/alerts/domain/event.go:95-110`) · `'alert_group'` as a persisted `subject_kind` in\n`db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:170,221` · `SubjectAlertGroup` · the external route\n`/api/v1/alert-groups` · OpenAPI and the generated client · 94 non-test files in total.\n\n**Scope.** large\n\n**Done when.**\n1. The table, its Go types, its module vocabulary and its API route no longer use the word `alert_group`.\n2. The name is chosen from what the object is FOR, decided only after `36bb67d` and `7178492` have landed —\n   those take the conversation key away from this object, and until then it is both a grouping of alerts and\n   the notification unit, which is why no name fits. If it remains the notification unit, the\n   `notification_*` family is right. If notification keys elsewhere and this becomes purely oto's grouping of\n   related alerts, it is not a notification table and must not claim to be.\n3. The six `group.*` event types are RETIRED, not deleted — up to 13 months of rows carry them. Follow\n   `00051` (`group.member_*`) and `00054` (`case.reopened`): the constant stays, stays decodable, stays in\n   the persisted-spellings expansion, and the writer refuses the old spelling.\n4. `subject_kind = 'alert_group'` is canonicalised on read the same way, in BOTH `notifications` and\n   `channel_threads`.\n5. The old route keeps working, or its removal is a declared breaking change with a version bump — it is an\n   external contract, and `00050` already refused to break it once for `receiver`.\n6. `docs/adr/0041-*.md`'s three-noun table is updated, and `test/scope/allocation_test.go` still enforces the\n   allocation under the new name.\n\n**Depends on.** `36bb67d` (subject kinds) and `7178492` (digest policies) — do not start before both land.","files":null}]}